he Hecke congruence subgroups Γ0(N) and Γ1(N) of level N are defined as\nL692@P12: Γ0(N)=\nL693@P12: ⎧\nL694@P12: ⎨\nL695@P12: ⎩\nL696@P12: γ ∈ SL2(Z),γ ≡\nL697@P12: ⎛\nL698@P12: ⎝ ∗∗\nL699@P12: 0 ∗\nL700@P12: ⎞\nL701@P12: ⎠ (mod N)\nL702@P12: ⎫\nL703@P12: ⎬\nL704@P12: ⎭\nL705@P12: and\nL706@P12: Γ1(N)=\nL707@P12: ⎧\nL708@P12: ⎨\nL709@P12: ⎩\nL710@P12: γ ∈ SL2(Z),γ ≡\nL711@P12: ⎛\nL712@P12: ⎝\nL713@P12: 1 ∗\nL714@P12: 01\nL715@P12: ⎞\nL716@P12: ⎠ (mod N)\nL717@P12: ⎫\nL718@P12: ⎬\nL719@P12: ⎭ ,\nL720@P12: respectively.\nL721@P12: One has the following inclusions as subgroups\nL722@P12: Γ(N) ⊂ Γ1(N) ⊂ Γ0(N) ⊂ SL2(Z),\nL723@P12: and each is a finite index subgroup of the succeeding one. The index of Γ(N) is given by the\nL724@P12: formula\nL725@P12: [SL2(Z) : Γ(N)] = N3 \u0017\nL726@P12: p/N\nL727@P12: \u0002\nL728@P12: 1 − 1\nL729@P12: p2\nL730@P12: \u0003\nL731@P12: and Γ(N) is normal in SL2(Z). All these facts are proved in [32]. Note that γ ∈ Γ(N) for\nL732@P12: N> 3 satisfies |Trace(γ)|≥ 2, so Γ(N) \\ H has no elliptic points and hence, viewing Γ(N) \\ H\nL733@P12: as a manifold in R3, it is a smooth manifold. We caution the reader that Γ(N) \\ H does not\nL734@P12: embed isometrically in R3 as the Gauss curvature of Γ(N) \\ H is −1 and thus the Efimov–\nL735@P12: Hilbert theorem precludes such an isometric embedding into R3 as Γ(N) \\ H is also complete.\nL736@P12: We already introduced the concept of fundamental domains and their description for Fuchsian\nL737@P12: groups at the beginning of this section. A picture of the Ford fundamental domain F for Γ(2)\nL738@P12: is displayed in Figure 2.\nL739@P12: F\nL740@P12: B A\nL741@P12: −1 −1/2 1/2 1\nL742@P12: Figure 2 Ford fundamental domain F for Γ(2). The circles |1 ± 2z| = 1 seen here are isometric\nL743@P12: circles, and Γ(2) \\ H is seen to have 4 cusps at z = 0, z = ±1 and z = ∞, but z = ±1 are equivalent.\nL744@P12: A, B are elliptic points of order 2.\nL745@P12: Since there are three non-equivalent cusps in the fundamental domain for Γ(2), the correspond\u0002ing scattering matrix (ϕab(s)) is 3×3. For more on fundamental domains the reader can consult\nL746@P12: [20] or [19, Chapter 2]. We have the following formula for the scattering matrix for congruence\nL747@P12-13: subgroups Γ(N) due to [14, 16] and [19, Chapter 2].1000 Cakoni F. and Chanillo S.\nL748@P13: Theorem 2.8 For the cusp a = ∞ and Γ = Γ(N) we have that\nL749@P13: ϕ∞∞(s)= π1/2 Γ(s − 1\nL750@P13: 2 )\nL751@P13: Γ(s)\nL752@P13: ζ(2s − 1)\nL753@P13: ζ(2s)\nL754@P13: ϕ(N)\nL755@P13: N4s\nL756@P13: \u0017\nL757@P13: p/N\nL758@P13: \u0002\nL759@P13: 1 − 1\nL760@P13: p2s\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nDLMF: §27.4 Euler Products and Dirichlet Series ‣ Multiplicative Number Theory ‣ Chapter 27 Functions of Number Theory (https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.4)\nciteturn137view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://dlmf.nist.gov/27.4\",\"lineno\":145}); Total lines: 202\nL121: Symbols:\nL122:     cite48†\\mu \\left(n\\right): Möbius function , cite37†\\zeta \\left(s\\right): Riemann zeta function , cite38†\\mathrm{\\Re }: real part and cite24†n: positive integer L123: Permalink:\nL124:     cite63†http://dlmf.nist.gov/27.4.E8 L125: Encodings:\nL126:     cite64†TeX , cite65†pMML , cite66†png L127: See also:\nL128:     Annotations for cite31†§27.4 and cite23†Ch.27 L129: 27.4.9  |  | {\\displaystyle \\sum _{n=1}^{\\mathrm{\\infty }}}{2}^{\\nu \\left(n\\right)}{n}^{-s}  | ={\\displaystyle \\frac{{(\\zeta \\left(s\\right))}^{2}}{\\zeta \\left(2s\\right)}},  |\nL130: \\mathrm{\\Re }s>1,\nL131: ⓘ\nL132: Symbols:\nL133:     cite37†\\zeta \\left(s\\right): Riemann zeta function , cite67†\\nu \\left(n\\right): number of distinct primes dividing a number , cite38†\\mathrm{\\Re }: real part and cite24†n: positive integer L134: Permalink:\nL135:     cite68†http://dlmf.nist.gov/27.4.E9 L13